 published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Mississippi sits among the most Republican states in presidential elections, with a partisan lean of roughly eleven points toward the GOP. No public polls of the race or expert ratings have been published, so the forecast rests on the state's fundamentals. The term-limited incumbent leaves an open field, but open presidential races rarely move a state's underlying partisan balance.\n\nNational coverage hints at Democratic strength in Republican-leaning states, but nothing Mississippi-specific has emerged to justify shifting away from that lean. The Republican nominee begins as a clear favorite; a Democratic win would likely require a substantial national wave that also carries other Deep South states. With two years until the election, both parties' nominations remain unsettled, adding uncertainty to who tops each ticket.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1069, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1631, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.153, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1578, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.153, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1586, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.1588, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.158, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1552, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.1593, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.1587, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.1556, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.1596, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.1637, "margin": -11.0}]}